{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Most array problems have an obvious solution built from nested loops: check every element against every other element. It is correct, easy to write, and costs **O(N²)** — which becomes unusable somewhere around a hundred thousand elements."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The two pointer pattern is the most common way to collapse that quadratic work into a single pass."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Instead of one index scanning the array, keep **two** — and move them according to what you learn as you go. Because each pointer only ever moves in one direction and never resets, together they take at most N steps."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/5092f410-eaa7-4301-a2ad-e74d30fad564.svg",
      "alt": "Two pointers starting at opposite ends of an array and moving toward each other",
      "caption": "Two pointers start at the ends and walk toward each other until they meet",
      "width": 880,
      "height": 290,
