    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "That is the entire trick: two indices, each moving monotonically, examining the array once between them."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why it beats nested loops"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A nested loop restarts the inner index for every value of the outer one — that restart is what makes it quadratic. Two pointers never restart. Each step permanently rules out a possibility, so no work is ever repeated."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The cost drops from O(N²) to **O(N)**, using **O(1)** extra memory. For a million elements that is the difference between a trillion operations and a million."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "When it applies"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Two pointers works when you can look at the current pair and confidently decide **which pointer to move** — without needing to check anything else. That certainty usually comes from one of these:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**The array is sorted**, so a comparison tells you which direction improves the answer.",
        "**You want symmetry**, like a palindrome, where the ends must match each other.",
        "**You are partitioning**, sweeping items into two groups in one pass.",
        "**A window is growing and shrinking** — the sliding window, which is the next chapter and is really two pointers in disguise."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The three shapes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The pattern appears in three arrangements, and the next lessons take one each:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "**Direct** — pointers start at the two ends and converge. Reversal, palindromes.",
        "**Reduction** — a sorted array, where each comparison eliminates a whole row of possibilities.",
        "**Subproblem** — fix one element, then run two pointers on the rest. This is how quadratic beats cubic."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If you can recognise which shape a problem wants, the code writes itself."
    }
  ],
